{
 "result": "csvsum.py is a single-pass pure-Python reader: it reads stdin as bytes, splits on newlines, skips the header, splits each row on commas, accumulates float(val) into a dict keyed by the raw category bytes, then sorts the categories and writes '%.2f' sums. It matches the sample output (diff SAME on in-0.txt, about 0.34 s on 1M rows) and the baseline is registered by assay at 1.99598 (noise 9.3%). I stopped here rather than spend energy on variants: the remaining gains I can see (splitlines, avoiding per-row checks) are likely under the 9.3% noise bar, so a win is unlikely to be measurable.",
